## Deep Reds and Berries: The Fall Pedicure Classic

Merlot. Oxblood. A red so dark it edges into plum before it edges into black. This is the family that never leaves the fall pedicure conversation, and for good reason. It reads as intentional in a way brighter reds don't, closer to a good glass of wine than a fire engine.

<strong>Fall pedicure colors</strong> in the red and berry family work because they carry weight without needing shimmer to do it. A rouge noir toe, the kind that looks nearly black until the light hits it directly, pairs with everything from denim to a slip dress. Oxblood leans slightly warmer and suits anyone who wants red's confidence without red's brightness. Deep plum sits one shade cooler again, closer to the merlot end, and flatters almost every skin tone because the depth does the flattering, not the hue itself.

Apply this family in two to three thin coats for full opacity, since a berry this dark shows streaks fast if rushed. A glossy top coat is non-negotiable here. Matte kills the richness that makes this family worth choosing in the first place. If a manicure in the same family appeals to you too, <a href="/blog/fall/burgundy-fall-nails">burgundy nails for fall</a> covers the matching finger version shade for shade.

