## Chocolate Brown and Espresso Tones for Toes

Think of espresso brown as black's warmer, more wearable cousin. It carries the same weight and confidence a true black would, minus the starkness. On toes specifically, this matters even more than on fingers, since a stark black pedicure can look severe against bare skin in a way espresso simply doesn't.

Espresso brown pairs with the same clothes chocolate brown does, meaning almost everything in a typical fall wardrobe, but it sits a shade darker and glossier. Chocolate glazed toes, the milkier, creamier cousin of straight chocolate brown, split the difference between neutral and statement. This is the shade to reach for if you want something that photographs beautifully in sandals during an unseasonably warm October afternoon and still looks right the day boots come out for good.

Both shades are forgiving on regrowth, a real advantage for a pedicure that has to survive four to six weeks between appointments. The depth camouflages the line where new nail meets old polish far longer than a pale shade would. Pair chocolate or espresso with a slightly glossy finish rather than full matte. Matte can flatten these tones into looking muddy instead of rich. For the matching manicure version of this exact palette, <a href="/blog/fall/brown-nails-fall">brown nails fall</a> has the finger-focused breakdown.

