## Gel vs. Regular Polish: What Lasts Longer This Fall

For <strong>fall pedicure colors</strong>, a gel pedicure typically lasts two to four weeks, while regular lacquer holds for about one to two weeks before chipping starts. The gap exists because gel polish is cured under a UV or LED lamp rather than left to air dry, which hardens it into a far more chip-resistant finish from the moment you leave the chair.

Toenails also work in gel's favor in a way fingernails don't. Toenails grow roughly half as fast as fingernails and take far less daily wear, since feet aren't constantly washing dishes or typing. That combination is why a gel pedicure regularly outlasts a gel manicure by a meaningful margin, sometimes stretching toward the month mark with the right aftercare.

Regular polish still has its place. It's gentler on the nail bed long-term, easier to touch up at home between salon visits, and doesn't require the same removal process. For anyone doing a lot of activity this fall, hiking, running, regular gym sessions, gel's durability tends to win out. For a quick seasonal refresh you don't mind redoing every couple of weeks, regular lacquer does the job without the extra commitment.

